      <span class="featured-label">Ongoing Research</span>
      <h3>Resolving Intent-Content Conflict in Retrieval</h3>
      <p class="featured-advisor">With Dr. Ali Al-Juboori, Ramapo College</p>
      <div class="featured-prose">
        <p>What happens when someone wants a happy movie about war? Retrieval systems break because they treat user intent and content semantics as the same signal. I'm building a framework that makes emotions first-class entities in a knowledge graph so the system can hold both ideas at once.</p>
        <p>1,600 queries tested. In conflict scenarios, the framework reduces affective displacement error by 0.037 vs. baseline. Edge-removal causal interventions confirmed the graph structure is doing real work.</p>
      </div>
      <a href="https://github.com/Prashant002-1/knowledge-graph-retrieval-with-affective-grounding" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="featured-link">See the code &rarr;</a>
    </div>

    <!-- Project cards -->
    <div class="project-grid">

      <a href="#" class="project-card glass glass-hover reveal">
        <h3>Canvas Agent</h3>
        <p class="project-stack">TypeScript, Next.js, PostgreSQL, pgvector</p>
        <p>I wanted an AI agent that could handle my LMS for me. Nine typed tools, persistent memory, a planner that knows what's due before I ask. The judges at HackRamapo responded to the architecture and documentation more than anything.</p>
      </a>

      <a href="#" class="project-card glass glass-hover reveal">
        <h3>Center for Food Action</h3>
